n (-2).  \n- Opponent has checked through — they may be weak too.  \n- A small raise (bluff) might work if they also have nothing, but given the board texture and lack of draws completed obviously, it's risky.  \n- But: **You checked last round, they checked — likely both waiting to see showdown.**  \n- If you raise now, it looks suspicious because the board didn’t change — why bet now unless strong? But you’re not strong.  \n- Best approach: **Check behind and show down cheaply.**\n\nReason:  \nYou have a very weak hand with little to no showdown value. However, since both players checked through all streets, your opponent likely has a weak or marginal hand as well. Raising would be a pure bluff with near-zero fold equity, given the passive history. Folding wastes the chance to see if they also have nothing and you win via board. Checking behind preserves chips and sees the showdown. Going all-in is overly aggressive and unjustified. Thus, **checking (0)** is safest and optimal.\n\nAnswer: 0"}
