an a single pair (if that), or a weak ace (e.g., A-2, A-3, A-5). Given that, **you can credibly represent strength now on the river** by making a small bet as a bluff.\n\nYour valid actions include checking (0), folding (-1), calling (not applicable since no bet), or raising/betting (minimum 100, or more).\n\nSince both players have checked to you on the river, **you have the option to bet (min 100)** or check back (0).\n\nIf you check, you almost certainly lose, unless opponent also has pure air and you somehow win with Queen-high — extremely unlikely.\n\nBut if you **bet**, you may induce a fold from weaker aces (A-3, A-5, A-2), or bottom pairs, especially since the board looks scary (three hearts, connected low cards). A well-timed bluff here has decent fold equity.\n\nGiven your chip stack (19900), betting 100 is only 0.5% of your stack — a very low-risk bluff with plausible deniability.\n\nAlternatively, going all-in (-2) would be overkill and less credible — unlikely to get a call from worse or a fold from better, and wastes chips if called.\n\nThus, the most promising move is a **small bluff bet of 100**, representing a completed flush draw or a straight.\n\nReason:  \nThe opponent has shown extreme passivity across all four rounds, suggesting a weak range. Your hand has no showdown value, so checking guarantees a loss if the opponent has any piece of the board. A minimal bet of 100 can fold out worse one-pair hands or weak aces, leveraging fold equity without risking much.\n\nAnswer: 100'}
